7. Hypergraph Robustness and Mission Interdependence

Missions often involve overlapping communities and resources, better described by hypergraphs than simple graphs. We model missions as hyperedges and agents as vertices; the hypergraph captures higher‑order interactions.

Following Bianconi & Dorogovtsev (2014), we examine percolation on a random hypergraph where nodes (agents) fail with probability \(q\). A mission (hyperedge) is considered active only if all its constituent agents remain engaged. The giant component of active missions corresponds to a resilient mission ecosystem.

**Theorem 5 (Hypergraph percolation threshold).** For a random hypergraph with hyperedge size distribution \(Q(s)\) and node membership distribution, the system loses its giant component when the fraction of removed nodes exceeds

\[
q_c = 1 - \frac{1}{\langle s(s-1) \rangle / \langle s \rangle},
\]

where averages are over the hyperedge size distribution.

*Proof.* The generating function formalism for hypergraphs (Bianconi & Dorogovtsev, 2014) maps the problem to a bipartite graph percolation. The condition for a giant component is analogous to that for ordinary graphs but with a modified branching factor that accounts for the hyperedge internal connectivity. ∎

This theorem provides a mathematically rigorous version of the “mission resilience” conjecture. It implies that diverse, overlapping mission structures (large hyperedges) increase robustness, as the critical removal fraction \(q_c\) becomes larger.

