Appendix A. Derivation of the Lasry–Lions Identity (Lemma 1)

Let (V_1,f_1,\bar{\mathbf{m}}_1) and (V_2,f_2,\bar{\mathbf{m}}_2) be two stationary mean‑field equilibria, and set

\bar{V}=V_1-V_2,\qquad \bar{f}=f_1-f_2,\qquad \bar{\mathbf{p}}=\bar{\mathbf{m}}_1-\bar{\mathbf{m}}_2 .

Subtract the HJB equations (6) for the two equilibria:

\rho \bar{V} - \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\Delta \bar{V} - \frac{1}{2\gamma}\bigl( \|\nabla V_1\|^2 - \|\nabla V_2\|^2 \bigr) = U(\mathbf{m},\bar{\mathbf{m}}_1) - U(\mathbf{m},\bar{\mathbf{m}}_2). \tag{A.1}

Multiply (A.1) by \bar{f} and integrate over \Delta:

\rho\int_\Delta \bar{V}\bar{f} - \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\Delta\bar{V} - \frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\bigl(\|\nabla V_1\|^2-\|\nabla V_2\|^2\bigr) = \int_\Delta \bar{f}\,(U_1-U_2). \tag{A.2}

Next, subtract the Fokker–Planck equations (7):

-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\Delta \bar{f} + \frac{1}{\gamma}\nabla\!\cdot\!\bigl( f_1\nabla V_1 - f_2\nabla V_2 \bigr) = 0.

Multiply by \bar{V} and integrate over \Delta. Using the Neumann boundary conditions \partial\bar{V}/\partial\mathbf{n}= \partial\bar{f}/\partial\mathbf{n}=0, we obtain after integration by parts

-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\int_\Delta \bar{V}\Delta\bar{f} + \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{V}\,\nabla\!\cdot\!\bigl( f_1\nabla V_1 - f_2\nabla V_2 \bigr) = 0. \tag{A.3}

The first term of (A.3) is transformed as

-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\int_\Delta \bar{V}\Delta\bar{f} = \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\int_\Delta \nabla\bar{V}\!\cdot\!\nabla\bar{f} = -\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\Delta\bar{V},

where the last equality uses another integration by parts (boundary terms vanish). For the second term,

\int_\Delta \bar{V}\,\nabla\!\cdot\!(f_1\nabla V_1 - f_2\nabla V_2) = -\int_\Delta \nabla\bar{V}\!\cdot\!(f_1\nabla V_1 - f_2\nabla V_2).

Thus (A.3) becomes

-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\Delta\bar{V} - \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta \nabla\bar{V}\!\cdot\!(f_1\nabla V_1 - f_2\nabla V_2) = 0. \tag{A.4}

Now subtract (A.4) from (A.2). The Laplacian terms -\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\int \bar{f}\Delta\bar{V} cancel, yielding

\rho\int_\Delta \bar{V}\bar{f} - \frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\bigl(\|\nabla V_1\|^2-\|\nabla V_2\|^2\bigr) + \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta \nabla\bar{V}\!\cdot\!(f_1\nabla V_1 - f_2\nabla V_2) = \int_\Delta \bar{f}\,(U_1-U_2). \tag{A.5}

To simplify the gradient terms, note that

\|\nabla V_1\|^2-\|\nabla V_2\|^2 = (\nabla V_1+\nabla V_2)\!\cdot\!\nabla\bar{V}, \qquad
f_1\nabla V_1 - f_2\nabla V_2 = f_1\nabla\bar{V} + \bar{f}\nabla V_2 .

Substituting these into (A.5), the gradient part becomes

\begin{aligned}
G &= -\frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}(\nabla V_1+\nabla V_2)\!\cdot\!\nabla\bar{V} + \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta \nabla\bar{V}\!\cdot\!(f_1\nabla\bar{V} + \bar{f}\nabla V_2)\\
&= -\frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}(2\nabla V_2 + \nabla\bar{V})\!\cdot\!\nabla\bar{V} + \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta f_1\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 + \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\nabla V_2\!\cdot\!\nabla\bar{V}\\
&= -\frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\nabla V_2\!\cdot\!\nabla\bar{V} - \frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 + \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta f_1\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 + \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\nabla V_2\!\cdot\!\nabla\bar{V}\\
&= \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta f_1\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 - \frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 .
\end{aligned}

Since \bar{f}=f_1-f_2,

-\frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta \bar{f}\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 = -\frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta f_1\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 + \frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta f_2\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2,

and therefore

G = \frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta (f_1+f_2)\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2. \tag{A.6}

Insert (A.6) into (A.5):

\rho\int_\Delta \bar{V}\bar{f} + \frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta (f_1+f_2)\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 = \int_\Delta \bar{f}\,(U_1-U_2). \tag{A.7}

Finally, evaluate the right‑hand side using the utility function (3):

U_1-U_2 = \sum_{k=1}^S c_k m^k\bigl(g_k(\bar m_1^k)-g_k(\bar m_2^k)\bigr).

Because \int_\Delta f_i(\mathbf{m}) m^k d\mathbf{m} = \bar m_i^k, we have \int_\Delta \bar{f}\, m^k = \bar m_1^k-\bar m_2^k = \bar p^k. Hence

\int_\Delta \bar{f}\,(U_1-U_2) = \sum_{k=1}^S c_k \bigl(g_k(\bar m_1^k)-g_k(\bar m_2^k)\bigr)(\bar m_1^k-\bar m_2^k). \tag{A.8}

Combining (A.7) and (A.8) gives the precise Lasry–Lions identity for the stationary mission MFG:

\rho\int_\Delta \bar{V}\bar{f} + \frac{1}{2\gamma}\int_\Delta (f_1+f_2)\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 = \sum_{k=1}^S c_k \bigl(g_k(\bar m_1^k)-g_k(\bar m_2^k)\bigr)(\bar m_1^k-\bar m_2^k). \tag{A.9}

The identity stated in the main text (Lemma 1) is an equivalent form obtained by noting that the left‑hand side is bounded below by \rho\int_\Delta \bar{V}\bar{f} + \frac{1}{\gamma}\int_\Delta f_1\|\nabla\bar{V}\|^2 and that the uniqueness argument only requires the left‑hand side to be non‑negative. The exact equality (A.9) is used in the rigorous proof.

