Appendix B. Continuity of the Fixed‑Point Map (Theorem 1)

We prove that the map \mathcal{T}:\Delta\to\Delta defined in the proof of Theorem 1 is continuous.

For a fixed \mathbf{p}\in\Delta, let V_{\mathbf{p}} be the unique solution of (6) with right‑hand side U(\cdot,\mathbf{p}), and let f_{\mathbf{p}} be the solution of (7) with drift \frac{1}{\gamma}\nabla V_{\mathbf{p}}.

Lemma B.1. The map \mathbf{p}\mapsto V_{\mathbf{p}} is continuous from \Delta (with the Euclidean metric) into C^1(\overline{\Delta}).

Proof. The HJB equation (6) can be written as

-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\Delta V + \frac{1}{2\gamma}|\nabla V|^2 + \rho V = U(\mathbf{m},\mathbf{p}).

By Assumption (A2) the Hamiltonian is smooth and strictly convex. For any \mathbf{p},\mathbf{p}'\in\Delta, the difference of the right‑hand sides is bounded uniformly in \mathbf{m} by C\|\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{p}'\|. Standard Schauder estimates for fully nonlinear elliptic equations with Neumann boundary conditions (Gilbarg & Trudinger, Theorem 6.30; Cardaliaguet, Theorem 2.1.3) give

\|V_{\mathbf{p}} - V_{\mathbf{p}'}\|_{C^{2,\alpha}(\Delta)} \le C \|U(\cdot,\mathbf{p})-U(\cdot,\mathbf{p}')\|_{C^{\alpha}(\Delta)} \le C'\|\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{p}'\|,

which implies Lipschitz continuity. ∎

Lemma B.2. The map \mathbf{p}\mapsto f_{\mathbf{p}} is continuous from \Delta into L^1(\Delta).

Proof. The Fokker–Planck equation (7) is a linear uniformly elliptic equation in divergence form:

-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\Delta f + \frac{1}{\gamma}\nabla\!\cdot\!(f\nabla V_{\mathbf{p}}) = 0,\qquad \frac{\partial f}{\partial\mathbf{n}} = 0.

Equivalently, f_{\mathbf{p}} is the stationary density of the reflected diffusion

d\mathbf{m}_t = \frac{1}{\gamma}\nabla V_{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{m}_t)\,dt + \sigma\,d\mathbf{W}_t + d\mathbf{K}_t.

Because the drift \nabla V_{\mathbf{p}} depends continuously on \mathbf{p} in the C^1‑topology, the associated transition semigroups converge in the operator norm on L^1 (Bogachev, Krylov & Röckner, Theorem 9.2.4). The unique invariant measure f_{\mathbf{p}} therefore depends continuously on \mathbf{p} in L^1. ∎

The moment map \mathcal{T}(\mathbf{p}) = \int_\Delta \mathbf{m} f_{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{m})\,d\mathbf{m} is continuous because \mathbf{m} is bounded and f_{\mathbf{p}} is continuous in L^1. Since \Delta is compact and convex, Brouwer’s fixed‑point theorem yields a fixed point \bar{\mathbf{m}} = \mathcal{T}(\bar{\mathbf{m}}), completing the proof of existence.

