Appendix C. Formal Hydrodynamic Limit: Derivation of the Macroscopic PDE

We outline the formal asymptotic reduction that leads from the spatio‑temporal MFG to the reaction–diffusion system (9).

Consider agents distributed over a spatial domain \Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d. At each point x and time t, the population plays the local MFG; the mean engagement is now a field \mathbf{p}(x,t). The value function depends on x and \mathbf{m}: V(x,\mathbf{m},t). In the adiabatic regime, local equilibration is fast compared to spatial diffusion, so at each instant the local stationary HJB–FP system of Section 2 is satisfied with parameter \mathbf{p}(x,t). Under the small‑noise approximation and quadratic control cost, the optimal local drift is \frac{1}{\gamma}\nabla_{\mathbf{m}}V, and a moment closure (Kizilersü et al., 2020) applied to the spatial Fokker–Planck equation yields

\partial_t p^k + \nabla_x\!\cdot\!\mathbf{J}^k = \frac{1}{\gamma}\bigl( a_k - b p^k + c_k g_k(p^k) \bigr) + \text{higher‑order moments},

where \mathbf{J}^k is the spatial flux of mission k. Modelling the flux by Fick’s law \mathbf{J}^k = -D\nabla p^k with an effective diffusion constant D>0 (arising from imitation and communication between neighbouring locations) gives the macroscopic reaction–diffusion system

\partial_t p^k = D\Delta p^k + \frac{1}{\gamma}\bigl( a_k - b p^k + c_k g_k(p^k) \bigr). \tag{C.1}

This derivation is formal; a rigorous hydrodynamic limit for the multi‑population MFG with common noise remains an open problem. The system (C.1) is the phenomenological macroscopic model used in the main text.

