Appendix D. Proof of the Cascade Condition (Theorem 4)

We prove the cascade condition for a network model that emerges from the macroscopic description with sigmoidal social validation. Consider a random graph with degree distribution P(d) generated by the configuration model. Each node independently draws a threshold \tau from a distribution with cumulative distribution function F(\tau). A node adopts the mission when the fraction of its neighbours that are already adopters exceeds its threshold.

The early stage of a cascade initiated by a single seed is approximated by a branching process of vulnerable nodes—those that become active after being exposed to a single active neighbour. A node of degree d with threshold \tau is vulnerable exactly when 1/d \ge \tau. Hence, for a given degree d, the probability of being vulnerable is

v_d = F(1/d).

To compute the basic reproductive number R_0, we follow a randomly chosen edge. The probability that the node reached by this edge has degree d is d P(d)/\langle d\rangle (the edge‑biased distribution). This node is vulnerable with probability v_d, and if it is vulnerable it will activate and can expose its remaining d-1 neighbours. Therefore the expected number of newly activated neighbours from an active neighbour is

R_0 = \sum_{d} \frac{d P(d)}{\langle d\rangle}\, v_d \cdot (d-1) = \sum_{d} \frac{d(d-1)}{\langle d\rangle} P(d)\, v_d .

A global cascade occurs with positive probability if and only if R_0 > 1 (Gleeson & Cahalane, 2007).

For a deterministic fractional threshold \tau, we have v_d = 1 if 1/d \ge \tau (i.e. d \le \lfloor 1/\tau\rfloor) and 0 otherwise. Thus the cascade condition becomes

\sum_{d=1}^{\lfloor 1/\tau \rfloor} \frac{d(d-1)}{\langle d\rangle} P(d) > 1.

The simplified expression used in the main text, (1-\tau)\sum_d \frac{d(d-1)}{\langle d\rangle} P(d), is obtained when the threshold distribution is such that v_d \approx 1-\tau uniformly, a common approximation in mean‑field analyses. The rigorous condition retains the indicator function.

