Appendix E. Proof of the Hypergraph Percolation Threshold (Theorem 5)

We analyse the robustness of a mission ecosystem modelled as a random hypergraph. Agents are vertices, missions are hyperedges. A hyperedge of size s survives if all s of its agents remain engaged. After a fraction q of agents are removed independently (with retention probability p=1-q), a surviving hyperedge is present with probability p^s. The emergence of a giant connected component of surviving hyperedges marks the resilience threshold.

Consider a random hypergraph with hyperedge size distribution Q(s) and a large number of vertices. For analytical tractability we assume that vertex degrees (number of hyperedges an agent belongs to) are Poisson distributed with a sufficiently large mean, and that the bipartite agent‑mission graph is locally tree‑like. Under these mean‑field conditions, the branching factor for the spread of active hyperedges can be computed as follows.

Pick a random hyperedge; the probability that it survives is \sum_s Q(s) p^s. Condition on survival: the hyperedge size distribution among surviving hyperedges is proportional to p^s s Q(s). Following a surviving hyperedge of size s, each of its s agents is connected to other hyperedges. The expected number of additional surviving hyperedges reached via a single agent is proportional to the excess degree distribution of hyperedges. A standard generating‑function calculation (Bianconi & Dorogovtsev, 2014, Eqs. 13–15) shows that the basic reproductive number is

R_0 = p\,\frac{\langle s(s-1) \rangle}{\langle s \rangle},

where the averages are taken over the original hyperedge size distribution Q(s). The giant component of surviving missions exists exactly when R_0 > 1. Therefore the critical retention probability is

p_c = \frac{\langle s \rangle}{\langle s(s-1) \rangle},

and the critical fraction of removed agents is

q_c = 1 - p_c = 1 - \frac{1}{\langle s(s-1) \rangle / \langle s \rangle}.

This completes the proof of Theorem 5.

