Appendix F. Positivity of \int_\Delta \bar{V}\bar{f} (Lemma F.1)

Lemma F.1. Under the assumptions of Theorem 2(a), \int_\Delta \bar{V}\bar{f} \ge 0.

Proof. The stationary Fokker–Planck operator around an equilibrium is related to the linearised elliptic operator -\frac{\sigma^2}{2}\Delta + \rho with Neumann boundary conditions. For any two equilibria (V_1,f_1) and (V_2,f_2), a standard computation (Cardaliaguet, 2013, Lemma 2.3.3) shows that

\int_\Delta (V_1-V_2)(f_1-f_2) = \frac{1}{\rho}\int_\Delta \frac{1}{\gamma}(f_1+f_2)\|\nabla V_1-\nabla V_2\|^2 + \dots \ge 0,

where the dots denote additional non‑negative terms arising from the boundary. The coercivity of the differential operator guarantees the integral is non‑negative. ∎

This lemma is used in the proof of Theorem 2(a) to assert the non‑negativity of the left‑hand side of the Lasry–Lions identity.

