Appendix G. Linearised Stability Matrix and Spectral Analysis

We provide details for the linearisation leading to the stability matrix \mathbf{J} in equation (11) of the main text. The macroscopic model (10) for a well‑mixed system (D=0) possesses a symmetric fixed point \mathbf{p}^* with p^{k*}=p^*>0 for all k. The intrinsic growth term around this equilibrium yields a self‑regulation term -\mu \mathbf{I}, where \mu = r(2p^*/K-1) > 0. The off‑diagonal interactions contribute \sigma \mathbf{W} with \mathbf{W} having i.i.d. entries of mean 0 and variance 1 (zero diagonal). Hence the Jacobian is

\mathbf{J} = -\mu \mathbf{I} + \sigma \mathbf{W}.

The linearised dynamics are \frac{d\mathbf{u}}{dt} = \mathbf{J}\mathbf{u}. The eigenvalues of \mathbf{J} are -\mu + \lambda, where \lambda runs over the eigenvalues of \sigma\mathbf{W}. By the circular law (Lemma 2), the spectrum of \sigma\mathbf{W} is asymptotically contained in a disk of radius \sigma\sqrt{S} centred at the origin. The stability analysis in Section 5 follows directly.


